{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1472","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1472","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1472\",\"parent_revision\":\"cd517f277ce80bf973fb897d72126320fdc71a39\",\"root_revision\":\"b5c697287e6ab721849b4f6824e685b2128e4ffcae80e275a8818ade5b573cd1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen the hallucination-detector stop hook blocks a subagent's response (due to trigger language like \\\"may\\\", \\\"appears\\\", \\\"seems\\\"), the agent appears idle to the orchestrator with no output. This is indistinguishable from a crash or infinite loop. The orchestrator has no signal that a hook fired \u2014 it only sees silence followed by idle notifications. Observed in P1422 QG phase 4 (doc-drift-auditor): agent completed full audit, output was visible in tmux pane %15, but hook blocked delivery to orchestrator. Orchestrator killed and respawned the agent, wasting all completed work. Two systemic gaps: (1) no mechanism for orchestrator to distinguish hook-blocked from crashed, (2) no protocol to check tmux pane output before killing idle agents.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: Subagent hook output blocked by hallucination-detector is indistinguishable from crash to orchestrator\\nDate: 2026-04-23\\nGoal: Give the orchestrator a reliable signal distinguishing hook-blocked agent output from crash or infinite loop, preventing unnecessary re-spawn of completed work.\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. Hallucination-detector hook location | AVAILABLE | plugins/agent-orchestration/skills/agent-orchestration/hallucination-triggers.md documents triggers; actual stop hook behavior from .claude/hooks/\\n2. Stop hook signal surface | AVAILABLE | Stop hooks return JSON to the Claude Code CLI; the `reason` field can carry structured metadata \u2014 but no current hook writes a distinguishable blocked-vs-crashed signal\\n3. implement-feature orchestrator health-check protocol | AVAILABLE | plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md lines 100-128 Agent Health Check section (subject to re-verification)\\n4. tmux pane check protocol | DERIVABLE | pattern exists (orchestrator can capture-pane before killing) but is not documented in the health-check section\\n5. Hook metadata passthrough mechanism | DERIVABLE | requires verifying whether Claude Code CLI surfaces hook-returned JSON to the parent session or drops it\\n\\nAVAILABLE count: 3\\nDERIVABLE count: 2\\nMISSING count: 0\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED \u2014 root cause well-understood from incident report. Two fix approaches: (A) hooks signal differently, (B) orchestrator protocol updated to check tmux before kill.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **subagent hook output blocked by hallucination-detector is indistinguishable from crash to orchestrator** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: P1422 QG Phase 4 incident \u2014 2026-04-04. Agent completed work, hook blocked response, orchestrator respawned unnecessarily.\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-04\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-23)\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] When the hallucination-detector stop hook blocks agent output, the orchestrator receives a structured signal (e.g., hook exit code, JSON reason field, or environment variable) that distinguishes block from crash \u2014 not silence\\n- [ ] `implement-feature` SKILL.md Agent Health Check section (lines 100-128) includes a step: before killing an idle agent, check the tmux pane output for evidence of completed work\\n- [ ] The hallucination-detector hook writes a log entry to `.claude/context/hook-events.jsonl` when it blocks output, including: session_id, timestamp, trigger_phrase, and truncated_output_preview\\n- [ ] A new test or manual reproduction scenario confirms that an orchestrator following the updated protocol does NOT re-spawn an agent whose output was hook-blocked\\n- [ ] `uv run prek run --files` passes on any modified hook scripts\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n- `plugins/agent-orchestration/skills/agent-orchestration/hallucination-triggers.md` \u2014 hallucination trigger patterns\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 Agent Health Check section\\n- `.claude/hooks/` \u2014 stop hook implementation files\\n- `.claude/context/hook-events.jsonl` (new) \u2014 hook event log\\n\\n### Story\\n\\nAs an **orchestrator session running implement-feature**, I want **a signal that distinguishes a hook-blocked subagent from a crashed or hung one** so that **I do not kill and re-spawn an agent whose work was complete but whose output was suppressed by the hallucination-detector stop hook**.\\n\",\"digest\":\"9836556ba7fdfa83c8b93ad21015a9cbe7c60edf795431f823b12e2aca7e3a90\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPg44xg\"}"}